# Request membership

Most access to a Nocturne site starts with an invite: the owner sends a link and the person
accepts it. **Membership requests** turn that around. Someone who lands on the site can ask
the owner for access, and the owner decides. Asking grants nothing on its own.

It is **on by default**, and each site owner can switch it off.

## What the visitor sees

At the foot of the sign-in card there's a **Request membership** link, under the passkey and
social-login buttons — see the annotated sign-in screen on
[Authentication](/docs/authentication#what-your-users-see).

Following it opens a short dialog with a message box, so you can say who you are and why you
are asking, and a **Continue to Sign Up** button:

<Screenshot id="request-membership-dialog" />

A useful note is short and says who you are in terms the owner will recognise — the name
they know you by, and your connection to them. The owner sees the note next to your request
when they decide, so a blank one is simply harder to say yes to.

<Callout type="info" title="Nothing happens until the owner approves">
  Sending a request does not create access. Until the owner approves it and picks a role,
  you see nothing of their data.
</Callout>

## What the owner sees

The toggle and the requests themselves both live on **Settings > Sharing & Privacy**.

- **The toggle.** While it's on, your sign-in page carries the *Request membership* link.
  Switch it off and no one new can ask; anyone you have already approved is unaffected.
- **The review.** Requests are listed on that same page — there is no separate queue to go
  looking for. Each shows who asked, the note they wrote, and when. Approving means picking
  a **role** (and any limits, such as _Only last 24 hours_), which adds them as a member on
  the spot. Denying closes the request.

The roles are the same ones an invite uses, so see
[Member accounts and invites](/docs/sharing/members) for what each role can see and do, and
pick the narrowest one that covers what the person actually needs.

<Callout type="tip" title="Approve deliberately">
  A request tells you what someone says about themselves, and nothing more. If a request is
  unexpected, or the note doesn't match anyone you know, deny it — someone who genuinely
  needs access can ask again, or you can send them an invite directly.
</Callout>
